            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book synthesizes multiple passages to argue that 'meat' is not just complex doctrine, but the word of God put into practice. John 4:34 explicitly defines Jesus' 'meat' as doing the will of the Father and finishing His work. Luke 8:21 and Matt 12:50 equate doing the Father's will with hearing the word of God and doing it. Therefore, meat equals the word done. Hebrews 5:14 provides the mechanism: strong meat belongs to those who 'by reason of use' (practice/habit) have their senses exercised to discern. 1 Corinthians 3 supports this because the Corinthians were denied meat not due to intellectual inability, but behavioral disobedience ('ye are yet carnal'). The book convincingly demonstrates that milk and meat can be the 'same word' (Christ crucified, 1 Cor 2-3), differentiated by whether it is merely received (milk) or carried through to obedience (meat).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Meat symbolizes the word of God carried out in complete obedience ('doing the will'), rather than simply advanced or complex doctrinal information.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"Milk is the word received.\",\n    \"Meat is the same word as milk, carried through to complete obedience.\",\n    \"Meat involves exercising the senses to discern.\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter's use of Proverbs 30:33 ('the churning of milk bringeth forth butter') and Isaiah 7:15 to bridge milk and meat via 'butter' is typologically intriguing but relies on equating butter with meat. This step is not strictly necessary for the glossary definition, which stands firmly on John 4 and Hebrews 5.\",\n    \"The argument regarding unpointed Hebrew in Ezekiel 34:3 ('ye feed on the milk' vs 'ye eat the fat') is debatable as it leans on the LXX against the Masoretic pointing, though it is not part of the glossary entry.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"Meat in didactic contexts symbolizes advanced, complex, or deeper doctrinal teaching (such as the Melchizedek typology in Hebrews 5) meant for mature believers, while 'milk' symbolizes foundational principles. Jesus' use of 'meat' in John 4:34 ('My meat is to do the will of him...') is a distinct, non-didactic metaphor about spiritual sustenance and mission, unrelated to Paul and Hebrews' didactic milk/meat contrast.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"Meat in didactic contexts means advanced doctrinal instruction (solid food), distinguished from milk by its cognitive depth and complexity rather than by the act of obedience alone.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter denies that the didactic 'meat' in Paul and Hebrews is defined by John 4:34 as 'the word obeyed,' insisting instead that Hebrews 5 explicitly distinguishes milk and meat as different topics of instruction.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Hebrews 5:11-12 equates 'milk' with 'the first principles of the oracles of God' (topics listed in 6:1-2) and 'meat' with things 'hard to be uttered' (like Melchizedek), implying a difference in doctrinal complexity.\",\n    \"1 Corinthians 3:1-2 uses meat to describe what the Corinthians were not 'able to bear,' which in 1 Cor 2:6 is called 'wisdom spoken among them that are perfect,' suggesting deeper instruction beyond the basic cross.\",\n    \"John 4:34 uses the Greek word broma (food/meat) in a completely different context—Jesus' own sustenance in doing the Father's work—which has no didactic milk contrast. Forcing John 4 into Hebrews 5 conflates a general metaphor for sustenance with a specific pedagogical metaphor for maturity.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Unifies the scriptural definition of 'meat' across John, Paul, and Hebrews without needing separate, unrelated definitions for different contexts.\",\n    \"Explains the explicit mechanism in Hebrews 5:14: meat belongs to the mature precisely because they have 'by reason of use' (practice/action) exercised their senses. If meat were merely complex information, intellectual study would suffice, but Hebrews requires action.\",\n    \"Explains 1 Corinthians 3: why couldn't the Corinthians handle meat? Because of behavioral disobedience ('ye are carnal... envying, strife, divisions'), not intellectual deficiency.\",\n    \"Resolves the tension of how Paul could say he determined to know nothing but Christ crucified (1 Cor 2:2) yet still distinguish between milk for babes and wisdom for the mature (1 Cor 2:6, 3:2); the message is the same, but the state of the receiver (doing vs merely hearing) differs.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"More easily maps to the topical progression in Hebrews 5-6 (moving from first principles like repentance and resurrection to the Melchizedek priesthood).\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"John 4:34\",\n    \"Hebrews 5:14\",\n    \"Luke 8:21\",\n    \"1 Corinthians 3:1-3\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
